 "118742b33fefe44dd1f2d61224db83b6a930b4b464865d14cf06c0c7d7730350" "basis" 1571 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127967" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3x+4y-z-7=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "118742b33fefe44dd1f2d61224db83b6a930b4b464865d14cf06c0c7d7730350" "basis" 1571 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4936" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(9,-2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=3\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/example-4936.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3513ada3bef748824e0e9f4a7f141e3f67cdaab401ccada61f4e60a202d69" "basis" 1571 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4936" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 9,-2,3 \\rangle$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 3,-4,7 \\rangle.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =⟨9,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z-56 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/example-4936.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3513ada3bef748824e0e9f4a7f141e3f67cdaab401ccada61f4e60a202d69" "basis" 1571 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-1,4,8)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\dfrac{x+3}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}{3}=\\dfrac{z+2}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/example-4937.json" "file_sha256" "45aed9213052394e8e98892d1d1e4bc8dc8d0ee1f155b30363e95fd7a9b6912f" "basis" 1571 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -1,4,8 \\rangle.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =⟨-1,4,8⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z-33 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/example-4937.json" "file_sha256" "45aed9213052394e8e98892d1d1e4bc8dc8d0ee1f155b30363e95fd7a9b6912f" "basis" 1571 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the Cartesian equation $2x-y+z+13=0$ of the plane $\\Pi,$ find an equation of $\\Pi$ in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot\\mathbf{n}=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/example-4938.json" "file_sha256" "872f8b172637688bee49d5c145564650f68089d7422f4260e501425d94809a46" "basis" 1571 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, from the coefficients in equation of the plane, we get that the normal vector of $\\Pi$ is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2,-1,1 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\Pi$ for $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z+13 & =0 \\\\ z & =-2x+y-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0,$ we get $z=-13.$ So, the point $P(0,0,-13)$ lies on $\\Pi.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 0,0,-13 \\rangle.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-13⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,-1,1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/example-4938.json" "file_sha256" "872f8b172637688bee49d5c145564650f68089d7422f4260e501425d94809a46" "basis" 1571 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,-6)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle -3, 5, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 1, 3, -2 \\rangle.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/example-4939.json" "file_sha256" "8a04740a5b6ffabf41d062547c296d3ee3b4bb4eb46036d78240e502d340aa90" "basis" 1571 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,-6 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-16i-4j-14k \\\\  & =⟨-16,-4,-14⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =⟨-3,2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z-124 & =0 \\\\ 8x+2y+7z+62 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/example-4939.json" "file_sha256" "8a04740a5b6ffabf41d062547c296d3ee3b4bb4eb46036d78240e502d340aa90" "basis" 1571 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336358" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-4,3,5)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨2,-3,-6⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,2,-8⟩.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$y\\,+\\,z\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053060/state.json" "file_sha256" "cda44c647d3d5dc847ff041d49e347e47fcfafb0e1bb2de2daaa73b81fc6382a" "basis" 1778 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053060/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336358" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-4,3,5⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -8\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =36i+16j+4k \\\\  & =⟨36,16,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =⟨-4,3,5⟩\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =-76\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =-76 \\\\ 36x+16y+4z & =-76 \\\\ 36x+16y+4z+76 & =0 \\\\ 9x+4y+z+19 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053060/state.json" "file_sha256" "cda44c647d3d5dc847ff041d49e347e47fcfafb0e1bb2de2daaa73b81fc6382a" "basis" 1778 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053060/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336358" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053060/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a0ff5befc88cb0e6d2fc3acf82014f4117fbe4bb16105e0afabc5c7206f3472a" "basis" 1778 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053060/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336358" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053060/state.json" "file_sha256" "cda44c647d3d5dc847ff041d49e347e47fcfafb0e1bb2de2daaa73b81fc6382a" "basis" 1778 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053060/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336358" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053060/state.json" "file_sha256" "cda44c647d3d5dc847ff041d49e347e47fcfafb0e1bb2de2daaa73b81fc6382a" "basis" 1778 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053060/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336358" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053060/state.json" "file_sha256" "cda44c647d3d5dc847ff041d49e347e47fcfafb0e1bb2de2daaa73b81fc6382a" "basis" 1778 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053060/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336358" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053060/state.json" "file_sha256" "cda44c647d3d5dc847ff041d49e347e47fcfafb0e1bb2de2daaa73b81fc6382a" "basis" 1778 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053060/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336358" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053060/state.json" "file_sha256" "cda44c647d3d5dc847ff041d49e347e47fcfafb0e1bb2de2daaa73b81fc6382a" "basis" 1778 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053060/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336358" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "19"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053060/state.json" "file_sha256" "cda44c647d3d5dc847ff041d49e347e47fcfafb0e1bb2de2daaa73b81fc6382a" "basis" 1778 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053060/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-33142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(2,-5,1)$, $P(4,-2,3)$ and $Q(5,1,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c5f4860f0a2096688d50ea88de3bd89b53ea267c6d001e7f866203a89d58cb4" "basis" 2811 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-33142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨2,-5,1⟩,\\,p=⟨4,-2,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨5,1,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨4,-2,3⟩-⟨2,-5,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,2⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨5,1,0⟩-⟨2,-5,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,6,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-15i+8j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-15,8,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =⟨4,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =-67\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =-67 \\\\ -15x+8y+3z & =-67 \\\\ -15x+8y+3z+67 & =0 \\\\ 15x-8y-3z-67 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c5f4860f0a2096688d50ea88de3bd89b53ea267c6d001e7f866203a89d58cb4" "basis" 2811 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-33142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f4dfa0264a7b6da1ba9e8b933a43c195d9359a9a9beda6e5f88438a79bcbb6ae" "basis" 2811 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-33142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c5f4860f0a2096688d50ea88de3bd89b53ea267c6d001e7f866203a89d58cb4" "basis" 2811 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-33142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "15x-8y-3z+70=0"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c5f4860f0a2096688d50ea88de3bd89b53ea267c6d001e7f866203a89d58cb4" "basis" 2811 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-33142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "15x-8y-3z-67=0"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c5f4860f0a2096688d50ea88de3bd89b53ea267c6d001e7f866203a89d58cb4" "basis" 2811 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-33142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x-5y+z-70=0"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c5f4860f0a2096688d50ea88de3bd89b53ea267c6d001e7f866203a89d58cb4" "basis" 2811 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-33142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "4x-2y+3z=0"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c5f4860f0a2096688d50ea88de3bd89b53ea267c6d001e7f866203a89d58cb4" "basis" 2811 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-33142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "5x+y-67=0"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c5f4860f0a2096688d50ea88de3bd89b53ea267c6d001e7f866203a89d58cb4" "basis" 2811 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-33142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "15x-8y-3z-67=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c5f4860f0a2096688d50ea88de3bd89b53ea267c6d001e7f866203a89d58cb4" "basis" 2811 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the Cartesian equation $5x+2y+z-4=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-⟨0,0,$\n{{field-1}}\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨$\n{{field-2}} $,\\,2\\,,$\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c5f4860f0a2096688d50ea88de3bd89b53ea267c6d001e7f866203a89d58cb4" "basis" 2811 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨5,2,1⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2y+z-4 & =0 \\\\ z & =-5x-2y+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=4.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,4)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,4⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,4⟩)\\cdot ⟨5,\\,2\\,,1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c5f4860f0a2096688d50ea88de3bd89b53ea267c6d001e7f866203a89d58cb4" "basis" 2811 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :quest