"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c5f4860f0a2096688d50ea88de3bd89b53ea267c6d001e7f866203a89d58cb4" "basis" 2811 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336228" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c5f4860f0a2096688d50ea88de3bd89b53ea267c6d001e7f866203a89d58cb4" "basis" 2811 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336228" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c5f4860f0a2096688d50ea88de3bd89b53ea267c6d001e7f866203a89d58cb4" "basis" 2811 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14078229/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-127967" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-2,3,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=3i+4j-k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-127967" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-2,3,-1⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨3,4,-1⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ & =⟨-2,3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ & =7 \\\\ 3x+4y-z & =7 \\\\ 3x+4y-z-7 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-127967" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0db189b3cff8cd8060c55b4efb2753af77c7f1444ef0157432f523b1bafaa19"} {"question" "q-127967" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-127967" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3x+4y-z-7=0"] ["math" "3x-4y-z+9=0"] ["math" "4x+3y-z-11=0"] ["math" "4x-3y-z+13=0"] ["math" "x+3y-4z-17=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-127967" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3x+4y-z-7=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(5,0,-3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨2,4,-1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨5,0,-3⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨2,4,-1⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,4,-1⟩ & =⟨5,0,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,4,-1⟩ & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4,-1⟩ & =13 \\\\ 2x+4y-z & =13 \\\\ 2x+4y-z-13 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0db189b3cff8cd8060c55b4efb2753af77c7f1444ef0157432f523b1bafaa19"} {"question" "q-33067" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33067" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x+4y+z+5=0"] ["math" "2x+4y-z-13=0"] ["math" "5x+y-3z-13=0"] ["math" "5x-3z-13=0"] ["math" "x+y-z-18=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33067" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x+4y-z-13=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33926" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,0,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨2,0,1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33926" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨1,0,3⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨2,0,1⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ & =⟨1,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ & =5 \\\\ 2x+z & =5 \\\\ 2x+z-5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33926" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0db189b3cff8cd8060c55b4efb2753af77c7f1444ef0157432f523b1bafaa19"} {"question" "q-33926" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33926" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x+2y+z-5=0"] ["math" "2x+z-5=0"] ["math" "2x-5y+z=0"] ["math" "x+3z-5=0"] ["math" "x+y+3z-5=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33926" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x+z-5=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-70805" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-1,1,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=i+j+3k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-70805" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-1,1,-2⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨1,1,3⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,1,3⟩ & =⟨-1,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,1,3⟩ & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,3⟩ & =-6 \\\\ x+y+3z & =-6 \\\\ x+y+3z+6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-70805" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0db189b3cff8cd8060c55b4efb2753af77c7f1444ef0157432f523b1bafaa19"} {"question" "q-70805" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-70805" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-x+y-2z+6=0"] ["math" "x+y+3z+5=0"] ["math" "x+y+3z+6=0"] ["math" "x+y+3z+8=0"] ["math" "x-y+2z-5=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-70805" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+y+3z+6=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-330498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-2,4)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨0,3,-5⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-330498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨1,-2,4⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨0,3,-5⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =⟨1,-2,4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =-26 \\\\ 3y-5z & =-26 \\\\ 3y-5z+26 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-330498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0db189b3cff8cd8060c55b4efb2753af77c7f1444ef0157432f523b1bafaa19"} {"question" "q-330498" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-330498" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3x+y-5z+26=0"] ["math" "3y+5z+26=0"] ["math" "3y-5z+24=0"] ["math" "3y-5z+26=0"] ["math" "3y-5z-26=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-330498" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3y-5z+26=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,0,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨-3,0,4⟩.$ From the vectorial equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨3,-5,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨3,-5,2⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =⟨-3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =-1 \\\\ 3x-5y+2z & =-1 \\\\ 3x-5y+2z+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0db189b3cff8cd8060c55b4efb2753af77c7f1444ef0157432f523b1bafaa19"} {"question" "q-33162" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33162" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3x+4z+1=0"] ["math" "-3x+y+4z-1=0"] ["math" "-5x+3y+2z+1=0"] ["math" "3x-5y+2z+1=0"] ["math" "3x-5y+z+1=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33162" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3x-5y+2z+1=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33165" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(8,3,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\frac{x+2}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-2}{-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33165" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨8,3,-1⟩.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨4,3,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨4,3,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,3,-1⟩ & =⟨8,3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨4,3,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,3,-1⟩ & =42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨4,3,-1⟩ & =42 \\\\ 4x+3y-z & =42 \\\\ 4x+3y-z-42 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33165" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0db189b3cff8cd8060c55b4efb2753af77c7f1444ef0157432f523b1bafaa19"} {"question" "q-33165" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33165" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2x+y+2z-42=0"] ["math" "-2x+y+2z=0"] ["math" "4x+3y-z-42=0"] ["math" "4x+3y-z=0"] ["math" "8x+3y-z-42=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33165" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4x+3y-z-42=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,1,0)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=-2+4t \\\\ z=3+t\\end{aligned}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,1,0⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨0,4,1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨0,4,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ & =⟨2,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ & =4 \\\\ 4y+z & =4 \\\\ 4y+z-4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0db189b3cff8cd8060c55b4efb2753af77c7f1444ef0157432f523b1bafaa19"} {"question" "q-336213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4y+2z-4=0"] ["math" "4y+z-3=0"] ["math" "4y+z-4=0"] ["math" "4y-z-4=0"] ["math" "x+4y+z-4=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4y+z-4=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336111" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,4,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=-1+2t \\\\ y=5+3t \\\\ z=7-t\\end{aligned}.$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,x\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$y\\,-\\,z\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336111" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨-3,4,1⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨2,3,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨2,3,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ & =⟨-3,4,1⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ & =5 \\\\ 2x+3y-z & =5 \\\\ 2x+3y-z-5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336111" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0db189b3cff8cd8060c55b4efb2753af77c7f1444ef0157432f523b1bafaa19"} {"question" "q-336111" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336111" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336111" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336111" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336111" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336111" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-128376" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-5,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=3+t \\\\ y=t \\\\ z=7-t\\end{aligned}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-128376" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨1,-5,2⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨1,1,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨1,1,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ & =⟨1,-5,2⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ & =-6 \\\\ x+y-z & =-6 \\\\ x+y-z+6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-128376" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0db189b3cff8cd8060c55b4efb2753af77c7f1444ef0157432f523b1bafaa19"} {"question" "q-128376" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-128376" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-x-y+z+1=0"] ["math" "x+y+z-3=0"] ["math" "x+y-z+6=0"] ["math" "x-y+z-6=0"] ["math" "x-y-z+3=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-128376" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+y-z+6=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the Cartesian equation $5x-7y+4z+20=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-⟨0,0,$\n{{field-1}}\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨5,$\n{{field-2}}\n,\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨5,-7,4⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-7y+4z+20 & =0 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{5}{4}x+\\frac{7}{4}y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-5.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-5)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,-5⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-5⟩)\\cdot ⟨5,-7,4⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0db189b3cff8cd8060c55b4efb2753af77c7f1444ef0157432f523b1bafaa19"} {"question" "q-336225" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336225" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336225" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336225" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336225" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336225" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-128400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the Cartesian equation $7x+y-3=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-128400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨7,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x+y-3 & =0 \\\\ y & =3-7x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$, we get $y=3.$ Hence, the point $P(0,3,0)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,3,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,3,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨7,1,0⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-128400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0db189b3cff8cd8060c55b4efb2753af77c7f1444ef0157432f523b1bafaa19"} {"question" "q-128400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-128400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(r-⟨0,-3,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨7,-1,0⟩=0"] ["math" "(r-⟨0,-6,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨7,0,1⟩=0"] ["math" "(r-⟨0,3,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨7,1,0⟩=0"] ["math" "(r-⟨0,6,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,7,1⟩=0"] ["math" "(r-⟨0,9,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨-7,0,1⟩=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-128400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(r-⟨0,3,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨7,1,0⟩=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336239" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the Cartesian equation $9x-3y+6=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-⟨0,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,,0$\n\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨$\n{{field-2}}\n,\n{{field-3}}\n$,0⟩=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336239" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨9,-3,0⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-3y+6 & =0 \\\\ -3y & =-9x-6 \\\\ y & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$, we get $y=2.$ Hence, the point $P(0,2,0)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,2,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,2,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨9,-3,0⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336239" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0db189b3cff8cd8060c55b4efb2753af77c7f1444ef0157432f523b1bafaa19"} {"question" "q-336239" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336239" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336239" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336239" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336239" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336239" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the Cartesian equation $3x+y+2z+4=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-⟨0,0,$\n{{field-1}}\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨$ \n{{field-2}}\n$,1\\,,$\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨3,1,2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y+2z+4 & =0 \\\\ 2z & =-3x-y-4 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}y-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-2.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-2)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,-2⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-2⟩)\\cdot ⟨3,1,2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0db189b3cff8cd8060c55b4efb2753af77c7f1444ef0157432f523b1bafaa19"} {"question" "q-336220" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336220" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336220" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336220" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336220" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336220" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the Cartesian equation $5x+2y+z-4=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-⟨0,0,$\n{{field-1}}\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨$\n{{field-2}} $,\\,2\\,,$\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨5,2,1⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2y+z-4 & =0 \\\\ z & =-5x-2y+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=4.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,4)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,4⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,4⟩)\\cdot ⟨5,\\,2\\,,1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0db189b3cff8cd8060c55b4efb2753af77c7f1444ef0157432f523b1bafaa19"} {"question" "q-336228" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336228" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336228" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336228" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336228" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336228" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-326312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,4)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨1,-2,-5⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,1,-7⟩.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$y+z\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-326312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-3,2,4⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -7\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =19i+7j+k \\\\  & =⟨19,7,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =⟨-3,2,4⟩\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =-39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =-39 \\\\ 19x+7y+z & =-39 \\\\ 19x+7y+z+39 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-326312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0db189b3cff8cd8060c55b4efb2753af77c7f1444ef0157432f523b1bafaa19"} {"question" "q-326312" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-326312" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "19"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-326312" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-326312" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-326312" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-326312" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "39"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336450" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-4,3)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨1,-3,-2⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,4,-1⟩.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,+\\,y\\,+$\n{{field-2}} \n$z\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336450" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨2,-4,3⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =11i+1j+4k \\\\  & =⟨11,1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =⟨2,-4,3⟩\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =30 \\\\ 11x+y+4z & =30 \\\\ 11x+y+4z-30 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336450" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0db189b3cff8cd8060c55b4efb2753af77c7f1444ef0157432f523b1bafaa19"} {"question" "q-336450" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336450" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "11"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336450" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336450" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336450" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336450" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "30"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-70833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,0,-3)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨-3,2,0⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,0,1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-70833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨2,0,-3⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =2i+3j \\\\  & =⟨2,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ & =⟨2,0,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ & =4 \\\\ 2x+3y & =4 \\\\ 2x+3y-4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-70833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0db189b3cff8cd8060c55b4efb2753af77c7f1444ef0157432f523b1bafaa19"} {"question" "q-70833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-70833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x+3y-4=0"] ["math" "2x+3y=0"] ["math" "2x-3z+4=0"] ["math" "3x-2y-4=0"] ["math" "z-4=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-70833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x+3y-4=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336358" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-4,3,5)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨2,-3,-6⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,2,-8⟩.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$y\\,+\\,z\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336358" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-4,3,5⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -8\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =36i+16j+4k \\\\  & =⟨36,16,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =⟨-4,3,5⟩\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =-76\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =-76 \\\\ 36x+16y+4z & =-76 \\\\ 36x+16y+4z+76 & =0 \\\\ 9x+4y+z+19 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336358" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0db189b3cff8cd8060c55b4efb2753af77c7f1444ef0157432f523b1bafaa19"} {"question" "q-336358" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336358" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336358" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336358" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336358" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-336358" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "19"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(2,-5,1)$, $P(4,-2,3)$ and $Q(5,1,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨2,-5,1⟩,\\,p=⟨4,-2,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨5,1,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨4,-2,3⟩-⟨2,-5,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,2⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨5,1,0⟩-⟨2,-5,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,6,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-15i+8j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-15,8,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =⟨4,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =-67\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =-67 \\\\ -15x+8y+3z & =-67 \\\\ -15x+8y+3z+67 & =0 \\\\ 15x-8y-3z-67 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0db189b3cff8cd8060c55b4efb2753af77c7f1444ef0157432f523b1bafaa19"} {"question" "q-33142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "15x-8y-3z+70=0"] ["math" "15x-8y-3z-67=0"] ["math" "2x-5y+z-70=0"] ["math" "4x-2y+3z=0"] ["math" "5x+y-67=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "q-33142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "15x-8y-3z-67=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d63ade4556c8b98f8fa9e0aa1938497c671675233deea09ee255a58a06f89"} {"question" "e-4936" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(9,-2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=3\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/example-4936.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3513ada3bef748824e0e9f4a7f141e3f67cdaab401ccada61f4e60a202d69"} {"question" "e-4936" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 9,-2,3 \\rangle$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 3,-4,7 \\rangle.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =⟨9,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z-56 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/example-4936.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3513ada3bef748824e0e9f4a7f141e3f67cdaab401ccada61f4e60a202d69"} {"question" "e-4937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-1,4,8)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\dfrac{x+3}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}{3}=\\dfrac{z+2}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/example-4937.json" "file_sha256" "45aed9213052394e8e98892d1d1e4bc8dc8d0ee1f155b30363e95fd7a9b6912f"} {"question" "e-4937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -1,4,8 \\rangle.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =⟨-1,4,8⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z-33 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/example-4937.json" "file_sha256" "45aed9213052394e8e98892d1d1e4bc8dc8d0ee1f155b30363e95fd7a9b6912f"} {"question" "e-4938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the Cartesian equation $2x-y+z+13=0$ of the plane $\\Pi,$ find an equation of $\\Pi$ in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot\\mathbf{n}=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/example-4938.json" "file_sha256" "872f8b172637688bee49d5c145564650f68089d7422f4260e501425d94809a46"} {"question" "e-4938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, from the coefficients in equation of the plane, we get that the normal vector of $\\Pi$ is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2,-1,1 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\Pi$ for $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z+13 & =0 \\\\ z & =-2x+y-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0,$ we get $z=-13.$ So, the point $P(0,0,-13)$ lies on $\\Pi.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 0,0,-13 \\rangle.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-13⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,-1,1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/example-4938.json" "file_sha256" "872f8b172637688bee49d5c145564650f68089d7422f4260e501425d94809a46"} {"question" "e-4939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,-6)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle -3, 5, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 1, 3, -2 \\rangle.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/example-4939.json" "file_sha256" "8a04740a5b6ffabf41d062547c296d3ee3b4bb4eb46036d78240e502d340aa90"} {"question" "e-4939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,-6 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-16i-4j-14k \\\\  & =⟨-16,-4,-14⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =⟨-3,2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z-124 & =0 \\\\ 8x+2y+7z+62 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084216/example-4939.json" "file_sha256" "8a04740a5b6ffabf41d062547c296d3ee3b4bb4eb46036d78240e502d340aa90"}] "usage" {"e-4938" [] "q-33142" [] "q-33165" [] "q-127967" [] "e-4936" [] "q-330498" [] "q-336228" [] "e-4937" [] "e-4939" [] "q-336358" []} "basis" 2882 "retractions" [[17592186120170 :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(8, 3,-1)$ and is perpendic